WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.480
Container escape threat modeling examines isolation boundaries, privilege, host exposure, orchestration access, and blast radius.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:18.500
Containers share the host kernel, so security depends on the combined behavior of namespaces, cgroups, capabilities, filesystems, runtimes, nodes, and policy.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:28.800
Important assets include workloads, secrets, images, nodes, registries, clusters, storage, identities, and CI/CD systems.

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:40.140
Common risk conditions include privileged containers, excessive capabilities, broad host mounts, exposed runtime interfaces, vulnerable images, unsafe defaults, and excessive RBAC.

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:47.320
Kubernetes adds service accounts, admission, scheduling, secrets, network policy, and multi-tenant concerns.

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:57.380
A workload compromise becomes broader platform risk only through reachable permissions and dependencies, which is why explicit trust boundaries and blast-radius analysis matter.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:13.840
Layered defense combines non-root execution, dropped capabilities, read-only filesystems, seccomp, AppArmor or SELinux, pod-security policy, admission controls, hardened images, protected secrets, segmentation, node hardening, and carefully managed exceptions.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:18.220
Runtime, Kubernetes, and node telemetry support detection and response.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:21.880
The goal is not fear of containers or procedural escape research.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:33.220
It is secure design and controlled impact: prevent boundary failure where possible, detect suspicious behavior quickly, preserve evidence, contain safely, and recover from known-good infrastructure.
